WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.285
AI governance should be practical enough to support adoption.

00:00:03.285 --> 00:00:06.860
If governance is only a slogan or a long document that teams ignore,

00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:08.875
it will not protect the organization.

00:00:08.875 --> 00:00:13.720
A useful program defines approved use cases, data handling rules, model approval criteria,

00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:18.705
vendor review, access control expectations, logging requirements, evaluation standards,

00:00:18.705 --> 00:00:21.970
incident response, vulnerability management, and change control.

00:00:21.970 --> 00:00:22.995
Governance should be risk-based.

00:00:22.995 --> 00:00:26.880
A low-risk internal drafting assistant does not need the same controls as a system that

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:27.940
uses regulated data,

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:31.840
supports customer-facing decisions, or can trigger production workflows.

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:36.490
The right level of review depends on data sensitivity, user population, tool access,

00:00:36.490 --> 00:00:41.195
external dependencies, impact of errors, and the ability to detect and correct problems.

00:00:41.195 --> 00:00:43.760
Ownership is essential. Teams should know who approves models,

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:47.820
who maintains retrieval collections, who reviews tool permissions, who handles incidents,

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:51.980
who monitors cost and reliability, and who communicates changes to users.

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:52.795
Without ownership,

00:00:52.795 --> 00:00:56.790
a private AI platform can drift into unmanaged use even when the original design was

00:00:56.790 --> 00:00:57.360
careful.

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:01.680
Responsible rollout includes pilots, feedback loops, documented limitations, training,

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:03.195
and continuous review.

00:01:03.195 --> 00:01:07.240
Governance should make safe paths easier, not merely slow down every request.

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:08.825
Sovereign AI is not a one-time installation.

00:01:08.825 --> 00:01:12.650
It is a managed capability that must be maintained as models, data, vendors,

00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:15.450
infrastructure, and organizational needs change.

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:17.330
Governance should also produce evidence.

00:01:17.330 --> 00:01:20.985
Approval records, model inventories, data-flow notes, evaluation summaries,

00:01:20.985 --> 00:01:25.320
incident records, and change histories help teams answer reasonable questions later.

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:29.080
What changed, who approved it, what data was involved, and what controls were tested?

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:33.200
Those records make responsible AI operations easier to defend and improve.
